# the instruction-level builder: encodes into sections of a mink object, with labels, relaxation, relocations and its statement record
#
# a builder holds the sections of one object over one registered set and a
# selection. each section is a list of fragments, an instruction, data bytes,
# a zero fill or an alignment, and its cursor is its end, so switching keeps
# every section's place. labels are the handles a symbolic operand names, and
# a label is local until it is exposed with a binding, a visibility and a kind.
#
# the builder is one module per part, each below the ones that read it:
# refusal, state, trait, section, label, frame, layout, emit, encode and
# object, and this module forwards the surface of each.
#
# every fragment is written under a context, the object's or another the
# caller states over the same register file, narrower as a source dialect's
# `.option norvc` keeps instructions full length or wider as `.option arch,
# +zbb` admits what the object's selection lacks. its admission, its encoding,
# its shortest form, its relaxation and its code fill are that context's. what
# the object states as a whole is the object's: its architecture is entered by
# the object's context, its selection traits are the object's selection's, and
# a derived trait holds when the object's context or any context written under
# admits it. a convention trait is the caller's abi row's, a fixed one the
# architecture row's, and a source trait the source's or the set's default.
#
# the surface a consumer builds against is frozen: `make`, `traits`, `open`,
# `switch`, `label`, `define`, `expose`, `align`, `data`, `datum`, `finish`,
# `Size`, the typed `Refusal`, `cfi` with its frame calls and `ct_begin` and
# `ct_end`. it grows by new calls, never by changing these. masc owns every
# byte count after relaxation, so a consumer reads `Size`, the layout and the
# listing and never restates one.
#
# nothing here names an architecture or a format: the fill, the relaxations,
# the compressions, the bindings and the markers are the set's, and sections,
# symbols and relocations are mink's neutral rows.

